 ],
 "literal": "Even if they go on an inauspicious day, with birds blocking their way in the middle, / even if they enter at an improper time and speak in an inappropriate manner, / they are not those who return empty-handed, as they take their path, / where waterfalls roar with abundant sound, / those who have sung of Malaiyaṉ, who possesses greatness.",
 "translation": "Even if they set out on an unlucky day and birds block their path, even if they enter at an improper time and speak inelegant words, they never return empty-handed along their path—those who sing of Malaiyaṉ of great renown, whose mountains abound with the roar of cascading waterfalls.",
 "modern": "அபசகுனமான நாளில் புறப்பட்டு, பறவைகள் வழியை மறித்தாலும், தகுதியற்ற நேரத்தில் சென்று, முறையற்ற சொற்களைப் பேசினாலும், மிகுந்த பெருமை உடையவனும், ஓசையோடு ஆரவாரித்து விழும் அருவிகளைக் கொண்ட மலையக நாட்டுத் தலைவனுமாகிய மலையமான் திருமுடிக்காரியைப் பாடிச் செல்பவர்கள், தங்கள் வழியில் வெறும் கையோடு திரும்பிப் போக மாட்டார்கள்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is addressed to the chieftain Malaiyamāṉ Tirumuṭikkāri by the poet Kapilar under the theme of 'iyaṉ moḻi' (praising a patron's natural virtues) in the Paṭāṇ tiṇai. The poet extols the boundless generosity of Kāri, stating that bards who sing his praises never return empty-handed. Even if the singers set out on an unlucky day with bad omens like birds blocking their path, or if they arrive at an inconvenient hour and speak inappropriately, Kāri's grace is so immense that he still rewards them richly. The image of the roaring waterfalls on his mountain slopes reflects his overflowing and unstoppable generosity. Key words include 'puḷ' (bird/omen), 'vaṟitu' (empty-handed), and 'malaiyaṉ' (the mountain lord).",
